<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>DW - English for Africa</title><subtitle type="html">News, Analysis and Service from Germany and Europe - in 30 Languages</subtitle><id>https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss-en-africa</id><updated>2026-11-08T20:38:49Z</updated><author><name>rssync</name></author><link rel="self" href="rss-en-africa" type="application/atom+xml" /><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.dw.com/s-12756?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><link rel="via" href="https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss-en-africa" type="application/rss+xml" /><entry><title>Dozens killed after migrant boat sinks off Senegal</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:e7b9408f55367fb3f63737617b1a0ca68c2ffc6dc65bf7d7083250c70e3ebec3</id><updated>2026-11-08T20:38:43Z</updated><published>2026-11-08T19:12:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/dozens-killed-after-migrant-boat-sinks-off-senegal/a-79890883--2c9fd71b9b64.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/dozens-killed-after-migrant-boat-sinks-off-senegal/a-79890883?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">The incident occurred near Kebemer off the coast of northwestern Senegal. Authorities on El Hierro in the Canary Islands have declared an emergency after a marked increase in migrant boat arrivals.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>South Africa elections: ANC marks its worst-ever performance</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:21638d9fb1dc2787ab5f4cb651188c41005208f4b136fb37d1411b08b9199f6b</id><updated>2026-11-08T20:38:46Z</updated><published>2026-11-08T17:35:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/south-africa-elections-anc-marks-its-worst-ever-performance/a-79887482--98e867ce01e0.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/south-africa-elections-anc-marks-its-worst-ever-performance/a-79887482?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">The African National Congress, the party of late Nobel Prize winner Nelson Mandela, slumped to its worst-ever result in South Africa's municipal election. The voters blame the ANC for water and electricity shortages.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>Egypt train crash leaves several dead, over 100 injured</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:592b7477cc13ddcf00614371dcc54008f2514561d623ed71f62bedda99243b21</id><updated>2026-11-08T20:38:49Z</updated><published>2026-11-08T16:49:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/egypt-train-crash-leaves-several-dead-over-100-injured/a-79892916--0936ecd1e565.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/egypt-train-crash-leaves-several-dead-over-100-injured/a-79892916?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">A train derailed after colliding with another train south of Cairo, with authorities confirming several deaths and saying more than 100 passengers were injured. Over 50 ambulances were deployed to the scene.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>Ebola: Why is there still no vaccine?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:9c03b828ba4f5418d59d2708b1d7acda9259560bad3b2510db364e2623033c2f</id><updated>2026-11-08T20:37:55Z</updated><published>2026-11-07T21:03:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/ebola-why-is-there-still-no-vaccine/a-79888134--134cc892a91f.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/ebola-why-is-there-still-no-vaccine/a-79888134?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Ebola has been known about for decades, but there is still no vaccine against the Bundibugyo strain of the virus causing the current outbreak. New research raises hopes of protection against the various types of Ebola.</summary><category term="World" /></entry><entry><title>Zambia's opposition criticizes government restructuring and layoffs</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:598b5cfe94c726892306ac39e4a2396281d2f0f6c2faffc99f2898df85c1161e</id><updated>2026-11-08T20:37:55Z</updated><published>2026-11-06T13:28:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/zambia-s-opposition-criticizes-government-restructuring-and-layoffs/a-79875892--f6feaaea394e.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/zambia-s-opposition-criticizes-government-restructuring-and-layoffs/a-79875892?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Government-backed dismissals and restructuring are fueling debate in Zambia. Opposition politicians are questioning the transparency of the plans; officials say the changes are necessary.</summary><category term="World" /></entry><entry><title>Wawira Njiru, Kenya's school feeder and German Africa Prize recipient</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:38b244c1fe6603721cea95e20eab1058f07c888abb070023ed2435ccca160d98</id><updated>2026-11-08T20:37:55Z</updated><published>2026-11-04T10:53:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/wawira-njiru-kenya-s-school-feeder-and-german-africa-prize-recipient/a-79646537--47b719bb31e0.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/wawira-njiru-kenya-s-school-feeder-and-german-africa-prize-recipient/a-79646537?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Standing ovations in Berlin: Kenyan social entrepreneur Wawira Njiru received the German Africa Award for her school feeding programme, which now provides meals to 730,000 children every day.</summary><category term="World" /></entry><entry><title>Will renewed conflict derail Ethiopia's economic growth?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:a102b677a2e73eff4237197713f83d563ac813a66d55aafcdb1dbe82aa63160a</id><updated>2026-11-08T20:37:55Z</updated><published>2026-11-02T11:19:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/will-renewed-conflict-derail-ethiopia-s-economic-growth/a-79807060--aec9783cc542.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/will-renewed-conflict-derail-ethiopia-s-economic-growth/a-79807060?maca=en-rss-en-africa-8291-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Ethiopia's economy is growing strongly. But renewed fighting in the north of the BRICS member state could deter investment, strain public finances and push up the cost of living.</summary><category term="World" /></entry></feed>
